{
    "title": "Air Plan Approval; South Carolina; Second Planning Period Regional Haze Plan",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 30, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-30/pdf/C1-2025-22565.pdf",
    "summary": "Today\u2019s Federal Register brings a handful of practical updates: the Postal Service is tightening up how certain flat mail pallets are labeled and combined, and federal fishery managers are slightly reducing the next season\u2019s king mackerel catch for one gear type in south Florida. There\u2019s also a small correction to a prior air quality notice.\n\nFor mailers, printers, and logistics providers, the Postal Service is clarifying pallet rules for flat mail headed to local processing centers. The changes spell out how to label pallets that hold barcoded and nonbarcoded pieces and allow combined pallets of marketing mail, bound printed materials, and periodicals when they\u2019re all going to the same place. The aim is smoother sorting and transport. Large mailers and consolidators could see efficiency gains and fewer handling hiccups. On the flip side, shops will need to adjust software, labels, and training, and smaller mailers may feel the pinch of retooling workflows and the risk of mislabeling during the transition.\n\nOn the water, fishery managers are trimming next season\u2019s commercial catch for king mackerel taken with run-around gillnets in the Gulf\u2019s southern zone, off south Florida. Last season\u2019s landings were a little over the limit\u2014about 1,200 pounds\u2014so the coming season\u2019s quota is reduced by that amount. This supports the long-term health of the stock and helps keep the fishery on a steady path. Gillnet crews and buyers in that niche may face a slightly shorter season or less product, while other gear types aren\u2019t affected. Consumers are unlikely to see major price swings from this modest change.\n\nLastly, the Environmental Protection Agency issued a simple housekeeping fix to a South Carolina haze plan notice, removing a mistaken agency reference. It\u2019s an accuracy update with no practical impact.",
